- P-148 **The checker's speed comes right after its correctness.** A rung that changes the checker measures how the change affects the time that the checker needs. First the rung makes the change correct. Then it does a second pass that makes the checker faster. This pass must not change the checker's results: the checker accepts and refuses the same programs as before, with the same errors at the same places. Ideally the rung does this work itself, and its skeptic checks the time and the results, rather than a manual step after the landing. Built in the `fortress-repo` skill (`compiler.md`), the batch script's rung and skeptic prompts, and the batch manual.
  from: explorations/coordinator/positions-audit/trial/evidence/corrections.md, "P-148"; from: explorations/coordinator/positions-audit/trial/evidence/judgement.md, "P-148"; by: the correction trial's writer (Opus), proposed for his review; note: his "ideally" put back: at 2026-10-14T19:16:45.667Z he rephrased the entry: after each checker change, measure its effect on performance; after the correctness pass, follow with an optimization pass; ideally within the worker's rung and checked by the skeptic, not as a manual step after the landing. The rest kept. Its other sources: his yes at 19:06:22.989Z to the coordinator's 19:04:27.473Z question whether to record the priority in POSITIONS; his question at 19:18:46.953Z what "keeping every answer" means, after the coordinator's rewritten entry of 19:17:31.763Z; the coordinator's answer of 19:18:57.169Z is the source of the clause on the same results, which he then asked only to put in plainer words (19:20:20.984Z); and the same idea four days before, 2026-10-10T05:27:34.812Z, answering the coordinator's 04:07:38.892Z question where the fix should go: a part of the batch should have done this optimization.

- P-150 **No resident compiler is built.** One warm JVM that serves many Fortress commands, as Scala 2's `fsc` did, would save little: the suites already run in one JVM each, and the commands that workers type took 6.4 % of the workers' time, their start-up alone at most 1.8 %. So the speed work goes to the checker itself (P-148). It is not attempted again unless something significant changes.
